<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kapurthala State</h1><p class="st-meta">1772 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Kapurthala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ahluwalia Sikh state of the Doaba region of Punjab, known for its Francophile Maharaja Jagatjit Singh and his Versailles-style palace.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahluwalia-misl">Ahluwalia misl</a> and a commander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sikh-misls">Dal Khalsa</a>, briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1761 and took Kapurthala in the 1770s. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fateh-singh-ahluwalia">Fateh Singh Ahluwalia</a> was an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kapurthala sided with the British after the First Anglo-Sikh War and in 1857, gaining estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagatjit-singh">Jagatjit Singh</a> (1877–1949) travelled widely, admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagatjit-palace">Jagatjit Palace</a> modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. Unusually the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> ruler governed a majority-Muslim population, and the state was known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>, including a Moorish-style mosque he funded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kapurthala acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 amid Partition violence and in 1948 joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a>, which merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kapurthala-state.jpg" alt="Kapurthala State" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kapurthala_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1772 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kapurthala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jassa Singh Ahluwalia, Fateh Singh Ahluwalia, Jagatjit Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism; Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kapurthala</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=kapurthala-state" aria-label="Kapurthala State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6626 1035 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kapurthala State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6946 1291L7364 1601M6946 1291L7364 1602M6946 1291L7003 1288"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7003 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6946 1291h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6946 1291h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kapurthala. <a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=kapurthala-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
